  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "`ObservableDictionary` and `ObservableSortedDictionary` announce a removal made through the non-generic `IDictionary.Remove` once, where they announced it twice.\n`ReplaceRange` on `ObservableDictionary` and `ObservableSortedDictionary` compares the keys to remove by the dictionary's own comparer, so it accepts a new key which that comparer considers equal to one being removed, and removes a key named more than once under spellings the comparer considers equal only once.\n`ObservableConcurrentDictionary` announces what an operation actually did when it loses a race with another thread: an indexer set or `AddOrUpdate` whose key another thread removed before it could update announces the addition it made rather than a replacement, and a `GetOrAdd` whose addition another thread made first announces nothing and returns that thread's value.\n`ObservableRangeCollection`'s range operations follow the reentrancy rule of `ObservableCollection<T>`: a change made while more than one handler is being told of a change is refused with `InvalidOperationException`, as `Add` already was, and `RemoveAll` and `GetAndRemoveAll` finish correctly when a lone handler changes the collection while they run.\n`MoveRange` refuses an index outside the collection with `ArgumentOutOfRangeException` before changing anything, whichever way the collection raises its events; one way threw `IndexOutOfRangeException` and the other could move or lose an element before throwing.\n`ObservableRangeCollection`, `ObservableDictionary`, `ObservableSortedDictionary`, `ObservableConcurrentDictionary`, `NullableKeyDictionary` and `NullableKeySortedDictionary` ask their logger whether it writes traces before composing one, so a logger set to a level above trace no longer costs an array and boxed values for every change.\n`ObservableRangeCollection` announces an insertion of one element through `InsertRange` and a removal of one through `RemoveRange` without copying the element into a list or array first, copies any other collection given to `InsertRange` once rather than twice, and returns the elements `ReplaceRange` replaced without copying them again.\n`ObservableDictionary` and `ObservableSortedDictionary` announce `Count` as changed before the notifications describing a `ReplaceRange` which removes some keys and adds others, or a `Reset`, as they do for every other change, where they announced it after them.\n`ObservableSortedDictionary` announces a change of `Count` when `Reset` is given a dictionary of another size, an empty sorted dictionary included, and nothing when it is given one of the same size, where it announced one whenever it held elements beforehand and none when it held none.\n`ObservableRangeCollection.ReplaceRange` and `ReplaceAll` given a different number of elements than they replace deliver the replacement and then the removal or addition of the rest each while the collection is as that notification leaves it, announcing `Count` before the second, rather than delivering both once the whole change had been made.",
